<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-rearrangements-of-infinite-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Rearrangements of Infinite Series
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      While the commutativity and associativity of addition imply that the order in which one adds up a finite sequence of real numbers does not matter, the same is not always the case for infinite series of real numbers. We will see that the convergence/divergence behavior of certain sequences can be meaningfully affected by rearranging their terms, but other sequences cannot be tampered with in this way.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-positive-negative-part">
    <title>
      Positive/negative part
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      positive part
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      negative part
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> x^{ + } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the positive part of <m> x </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> x^{ - } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the negative part of <m> x </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <title>
            Positive part
          </title>
          
          <p>
            The <term> positive part </term> <m> x^{ + } </m> of a real number <m> x \in \R </m> is the non-negative real number
            
            <md>
              x^{ + } = \max \of{ \set{ x , 0 } }
            </md>.
            
            Similarly, the <term> positive part </term> of a series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> is the series
            
            <md>
              \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \max \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } }
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>
            Negative part
          </title>
          
          <p>
            The <term> negative part </term> <m> x^{ - } </m> of a real number <m> x \in \R </m> is the non-negative real number
            
            <md>
              x^{ - } = -\min \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } = \max \of{ \set{ -x , 0 } } = \of{ -x }^{ + }
            </md>.
            
            Similarly, the <term> negative part </term> of a series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> is the series
            
            <md>
              \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ -\min \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } }
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <p>
    The positive and negative parts of a real number are closely related to its absolute value.
  </p>
  
  <lemma xml:id="lemma-positive-negative-parts-and-magnitude">
    <title>
      Positive/negative parts and magnitude
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m> x = x^{ + } - x^{ - } </m> and <m> \abs{ x } = x^{ + } + x^{ - } </m> for any real number <m> x \in \R </m> with positive part <m> x^{ + } \geq 0 </m> and negative part <m> x^{ - } \leq 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        First suppose that <m> x = 0 </m>. Then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } \amp = \max \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } = \max \of{ \set{ 0 } } = 0
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            x^{ - } \amp = -\min \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } = -\min \of{ \set{ 0 } } = -0 = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and so
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } - x^{ - } \amp = 0 - 0 = 0 = x
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } + x^{ - } \amp = 0 + 0 = 0 = \abs{ 0 } = \abs{ x }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Now suppose that <m> x \gt 0 </m> is positive. Then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } \amp = \max \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } = x
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            x^{ - } \amp = -\min \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } = -0 = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and so
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } - x^{ - } \amp = x - 0 = x
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } + x^{ - } \amp = x + 0 = x = \abs{ x }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Now suppose that <m> x \lt 0 </m> is positive. Then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } \amp = \max \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } = 0
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            x^{ - } \amp = -\min \of{ \set{ x , 0 } } = -x
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and so
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } - x^{ - } \amp = 0 - \of{ -x } = x
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            x^{ + } + x^{ - } \amp = 0 + \of{ -x } = -x = \abs{ x }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </lemma>
  
  <p>
    If a series of real numbers converges, then the convergence or divergence behavior of its positive and negative parts are not unrelated. The following result establishes a dichotomy of behavior based on whether or not these related series converge or diverge.
  </p>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-absolute-conditional-series-convergence">
    <title>
      Absolute/conditional series convergence
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m>. If the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        converges, then its positive and negative parts either both converge, in which case the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } }
        </md>
        
        converges, or they both diverge to <m> \infty </m>, in which case the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } }
        </md>
        
        diverges to <m> \infty </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ P_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ N_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be the sequences of partial sums
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            P_{ n } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } \amp N_{ n } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ n }^{ - } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Since both <m> x^{ + } </m> and <m> x^{ - } </m> are non-negative for any real number <m> x \in \R </m>, we observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            P_{ n + 1 } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n + 1 }{ x_{ k }^{ + } } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ k }^{ + } } + x_{ n + 1 }^{ + } \geq P_{ n }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            N_{ n + 1 } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n + 1 }{ x_{ k }^{ - } } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ k }^{ - } } + x_{ n + 1 }^{ - } \geq N_{ n }
          </mrow>
        </md>;
        
        that is, the partial sums <m> \of{ P_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ N_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> are non-decreasing. The <xref ref="theorem-monotone-convergence-theorem" text="title"/> now implies that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ P_{ n } } \amp = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ P_{ n } } \amp \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ N_{ n } } \amp = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ N_{ n } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        In particular, each of the positive and negative parts <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } </m> and <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } } </m> either converges or diverges to <m> \infty </m>. It remains to show only that one cannot converge while the other diverges to <m> \infty </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        To that end, we first note that if the positive part <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } </m> converges, then <xref ref="lemma-positive-negative-parts-and-magnitude"/> and <xref ref="proposition-arithmetic-of-convergent-series"/> imply that
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } - x_{ n } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } - \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        converges, in which case
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } + x_{ n }^{ - } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } + \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } }
        </md>
        
        converges by another application of <xref ref="lemma-positive-negative-parts-and-magnitude"/> and (1) of <xref ref="proposition-arithmetic-of-convergent-series"/>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Finally, we note that if the negative part <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } } </m> converges, then <xref ref="lemma-positive-negative-parts-and-magnitude"/> and (1) of <xref ref="proposition-arithmetic-of-convergent-series"/> imply that
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } + x_{ n }^{ - } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } + \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } }
        </md>
        
        converges, in which case
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } + x_{ n }^{ - } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } + \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } }
        </md>
        
        converges by another application of <xref ref="lemma-positive-negative-parts-and-magnitude"/> and (1) of <xref ref="proposition-arithmetic-of-convergent-series"/>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  
  <p>
    The dichotomy produced by <xref ref="proposition-absolute-conditional-series-convergence"/> is important enough to be named. A convergent series is called <term> absolutely convergent </term> if its positive and negative parts converge, and <term> conditionally convergent </term> if its positive and negative parts diverge to <m> \infty </m>.
  </p>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-absolute-conditional-series-convergence">
    <title>
      Absolute/conditional series convergence
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      absolute convergence
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      conditional convergence
    </idx>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m>.
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>
              Absolute convergence
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> <term> converges absolutely </term> if any two of the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m>, <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } </m>, <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } } </m>, and <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } </m> converge, in which case they all converge.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Conditional convergence
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> <term> converges conditionally </term> if the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges but any of these series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ + } } </m>, <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n }^{ - } } </m>, and <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } </m> diverge to <m> \infty </m>, in which case they all diverge to <m> \infty </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    We will be able to provide many examples of conditionally convergent and absolutely convergent series after establishing more convergence tests in <xref ref="section-tests-for-series-convergence"/>.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    One of the foundational results in real analysis is concerned with the limiting behavior of conditionally convergent series. We present the following result without proof:
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-rearrangement-theorem">
    <title>
      Rearrangement theorem
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Bernhard Riemann
    </creator>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of real numbers <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m>, and suppose that the associated series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        converges.
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges absolutely, then
              
              <md>
                \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ \varphi \of{ n } } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } }
              </md>
              
              for all bijections <m> \varphi \colon \N \to \N </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges conditionally, then for any extended real numbers <m> -\infty \leq a \leq b \leq \infty </m>, there is a bijection <m> \varphi \colon \N \to \N </m> so that
              
              <md>
                <mrow>
                  \liminf_{ n \to \infty }{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ \varphi \of{ n } } } } \amp = a \amp \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ \varphi \of{ n } } } } \amp = b
                </mrow>
              </md>.
              
              In particular, for any extended real number <m> -\infty \leq c \leq \infty </m>, there is a bijection <m> \varphi \colon \N \to \N </m> so that
              
              <md>
                \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ \varphi \of{ n } } } = c
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      One way to interpret <xref ref="theorem-rearrangement-theorem" text="title"/> is that conditionally convergent series are only convergent because the terms are ordered in such a way to cancel in a particular way, and that we may change the convergence/divergence behavior of such a series in almost any way solely by rearranging the terms. On the other hand, the convergence behavior of absolutely convergent series is completely unaffected by rearrangements.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
